  "observations": [
    "Both pins restore exact outputs after deletion. Shared source and semantic environment changes invalidate; ignored passthrough and unrelated file changes reuse.",
    "A child input-policy edit invalidates only that child. Its later source edit also invalidates only that child, while root output inheritance remains effective.",
    "Legacy globalDependencies contributes the shared file to the global hash. With globalConfiguration, the file is in per-task inputs and absent from the global file hash.",
    "On both pins, the tested child negation is retained in the resolved definition but the shared file remains hashed; both tasks recompute after it changes. No exclusion speedup is asserted.",
    "Mixed legacy/root-global keys, a global block with the flag disabled, and a child global block all fail before output materialization on both pins."
  ],
  "decisionRationale": "The current root already uses the global namespace and all 26 child configurations avoid root-only global settings. Retain that enabled layout and its existing six global inputs. The native controls support this configuration boundary; the unsupported exclusion expectation gives no authority to narrow any real semantic input.",
  "expectedBenefit": "Preserve the existing reviewed configuration layout. No new production critical-path or cost benefit is measured by retaining it.",
  "qualificationGranted": false,
  "productionChanged": false,
  "limitations": [
    "Synthetic Linux workspace evidence only; actual task contracts and supported cross-host profiles still require qualification.",
    "The broader graph/global-and-child feature family and all whole-goal acceptance criteria remain pending.",
    "The original exclusion expectation failed; source, native summaries and bounded logs are retained privately. The final report asserts the observed conservative recomputation instead."
  ],
  "reopenWhen": [
    "A native client changes global-input projection or exclusion semantics.",
    "Any proposal removes a current global input, moves a global setting into child configuration, or changes the enabled flag.",
    "A named task or consumer requires a measured global-input narrowing optimization."
  ],
  "rollback": "No runtime setting changes. Revert this research decision and evidence registration while retaining original receipts; an actual global-layout migration needs its own reviewed source and native controls."
